<TutorialDetail Tutorials="TutorialSet.Events" Slug="07-services-and-background-work">
<p><strong>Goal:</strong> publish events and write state from code that isn't a component, such as a server service, a timer or a SignalR callback, and let components update safely.</p>

<p><em>See it running:</em> the <strong>Live dashboard</strong> sample page (<a href="/events/example/live-dashboard"><code>/events/example/live-dashboard</code></a>).</p>

<h2 id="whats-different-outside-components">What's different outside components</h2>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th></th><th>In a <code>RfEventComponent</code></th><th>In a service</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Get the hub</td><td><code>PublishAsync</code>, <code>State&lt;T&gt;()</code> helpers</td><td>Inject <code>IRfEventHub</code> (the root)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Subscribe</td><td><code>[Subscribe]</code>, <code>[UseState]</code></td><td><code>hub.Subscribe&lt;T&gt;(…)</code>, <code>store.Subscribe(…)</code>, and <strong>dispose the result</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Thread</td><td>The renderer's</td><td>Whatever thread you're on. <strong>Publishing from any thread is safe</strong>: components marshal through their own <code>InvokeAsync</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="1-define-what-the-service-produces">1. Define what the service produces</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Clock/ClockState.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Clock;

public sealed record ClockState(DateTime Now, bool Running) : IState&lt;ClockState&gt;
{
    public static ClockState Initial =&gt; new(DateTime.MinValue, false);
}

/// &lt;summary&gt;Published once per minute boundary.&lt;/summary&gt;
public sealed record MinutePassed(DateTime At) : IEvent;</code></pre>

<p>These live in the <strong>Client</strong> project, so components in any render mode can use them, even though the service that produces them is server-only.</p>

<h2 id="2-write-a-scoped-service-that-publishes-from-a-timer">2. Write a Scoped service that publishes from a timer</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp/Services/ClockService.cs
using MyApp.Client.Features.Clock;
using RForge.Blazor.Events;

namespace MyApp.Services;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Server-only, Scoped (one per circuit). Ticks on a thread-pool thread, never the renderer's.&lt;/summary&gt;
public sealed class ClockService(IRfEventHub hub) : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Lock _gate = new();
    private Timer? _timer;
    private int _lastMinute = -1;

    public void Start()
    {
        lock (_gate)
        {
            _timer ??= new Timer(_ =&gt; _ = TickAsync(), null, TimeSpan.Zero, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1));
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        lock (_gate)
        {
            _timer?.Dispose();
            _timer = null;
        }

        hub.State&lt;ClockState&gt;().Update(s =&gt; s with { Running = false });
    }

    private async Task TickAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var now = DateTime.Now;
            hub.State&lt;ClockState&gt;().Set(new ClockState(now, Running: true));

            if (now.Minute != _lastMinute)
            {
                _lastMinute = now.Minute;
                await hub.PublishAsync(new MinutePassed(now));
            }
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            // The circuit (and its hub) ended between ticks.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        lock (_gate)
        {
            _timer?.Dispose();
            _timer = null;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

<p>Register it as <strong>Scoped</strong>:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp/Program.cs (excerpt)
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;ClockService&gt;();</code></pre>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Never register a service that holds <code>IRfEventHub</code> as Singleton.</strong> On Blazor Server, the hub is per circuit, so a singleton would capture one user's hub and share it with everyone. In Development, DI scope validation reports this as an error at startup.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2 id="3-components-just-read">3. Components just read</h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Clock/Components/ClockFace.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;span class="font-monospace fs-3"&gt;@@(Clock.Running ? Clock.Now.ToString("HH:mm:ss") : "--:--:--")&lt;/span&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Clock/Components/ClockFace.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Clock.Components;

/// &lt;summary&gt;No InvokeAsync anywhere: the base class marshals updates from the timer thread.&lt;/summary&gt;
public partial class ClockFace
{
    [UseState]
    private partial ClockState Clock { get; }
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Clock/Components/MinuteLog.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;ul class="small"&gt;@@foreach (var at in _minutes) { &lt;li&gt;@@at.ToString("HH:mm")&lt;/li&gt; }&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Clock/Components/MinuteLog.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Clock.Components;

public partial class MinuteLog
{
    private readonly List&lt;DateTime&gt; _minutes = [];

    [Subscribe]
    private void OnMinutePassed(MinutePassed e) =&gt; _minutes.Insert(0, e.At);
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="4-start-and-stop-from-a-server-page">4. Start and stop from a server page</h2>

<p>The page injects the server-only service, so it lives in the server project and uses <code>InteractiveServer</code>:</p>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp/Components/Pages/ClockPage.razor *@@
@@page "/clock"
@@rendermode @@(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))
@@implements IDisposable
@@inject MyApp.Services.ClockService Clock
@@using MyApp.Client.Features.Clock.Components

&lt;ClockFace /&gt;
&lt;MinuteLog /&gt;

@@code {
    protected override void OnInitialized() =&gt; Clock.Start();

    public void Dispose() =&gt; Clock.Stop();
}</code></pre>

<p><code>prerender: false</code> keeps the timer from starting in the throwaway prerender scope (<a href="/events/tutorials/08-prerendering-and-persistence">tutorial 08</a>).</p>

<h2 id="5-subscribe-from-a-service">5. Subscribe from a service</h2>

<p>Services subscribe through <code>IRfEventHub</code> and must dispose the subscription:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp/Services/ClockAuditor.cs
using MyApp.Client.Features.Clock;
using RForge.Blazor.Events;

namespace MyApp.Services;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Scoped listener: logs every minute boundary for this circuit.&lt;/summary&gt;
public sealed class ClockAuditor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly IDisposable _subscription;

    public ClockAuditor(IRfEventHub hub, ILogger&lt;ClockAuditor&gt; logger) =&gt;
        _subscription = hub.Subscribe&lt;MinutePassed&gt;((e, cancellationToken) =&gt;
        {
            logger.LogInformation("Minute passed at {At}", e.At);
            return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
        });

    public void Dispose() =&gt; _subscription.Dispose();
}</code></pre>

<p>A Scoped service is only created when something resolves it. To have it listening for the whole circuit, resolve it early, for example with <code>@@inject ClockAuditor Auditor</code> in a page or layout component that's always interactive.</p>

<p>Service handlers run <strong>on the publisher's thread</strong> (no marshaling: no renderer is involved). Keep them thread-safe.</p>

<h2 id="6-decide-how-service-failures-are-reported">6. Decide how service failures are reported</h2>

<p>A throwing <strong>component</strong> handler always goes to its <code>ErrorBoundary</code>. For <strong>service</strong> handlers you choose:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/EventHubSetup.cs (excerpt)
public static void Configure(RfEventOptions options)
{
    options.HandlerErrorMode = HandlerErrorMode.Log;   // default: log and continue
    // options.HandlerErrorMode = HandlerErrorMode.Throw; // run all, then throw AggregateException to the publisher
    options.MaxPublishDepth = 16;                      // nested publishes allowed before RfEventReentrancyException
}</code></pre>

<p><code>MaxPublishDepth</code> catches event loops (A's handler publishes B, B's handler publishes A, …) and stops them with <code>RfEventReentrancyException</code> instead of hanging.</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

<ul>
<li><code>IRfEventHub</code> is the root scope of the current DI scope: per circuit on Server, per app instance in WebAssembly, per request when rendering statically.</li>
<li>Store writes are serialized per store (<code>Update</code> is atomic). Component notifications are queued through each component's renderer.</li>
<li><code>PublishAsync</code> awaits every handler, including component handlers running on the renderer, so a service can rely on "all reactions ran" after the <code>await</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="-best-practices">Best practices</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Put server-only services in the server project</strong> (<code>MyApp/Services/</code>), and keep the state and events they produce in the Client project.</li>
<li><strong>Register hub-using services as Scoped.</strong> Never Singleton.</li>
<li><strong>Prefer state for continuous data</strong> (prices, clocks, progress) and events for discrete moments (minute passed, job finished). State means a component that appears later still sees the current value.</li>
<li><strong>Catch <code>ObjectDisposedException</code> in timers.</strong> The circuit can end between ticks.</li>
<li><strong>Stop background work when its page goes away</strong> (<code>Dispose</code>/<code>DisposeAsyncCore</code>), and also in the service's own <code>Dispose</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Turn prerendering off</strong> for pages that start background work on initialization.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One user sees another user's updates</td><td>Service registered as Singleton</td><td>Register as Scoped</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>InvalidOperationException: … not associated with the Dispatcher</code></td><td>Calling <code>StateHasChanged</code> from a service callback yourself</td><td>Let the hub do it: write state or publish, and let components react</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timer keeps running after leaving the page</td><td>Not stopped on dispose</td><td>Stop it in the page's <code>Dispose</code> and in the service's <code>Dispose</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RfEventReentrancyException</code></td><td>Handlers publish events that trigger each other</td><td>Break the loop, or check whether the change is needed before publishing</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

